{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cf6b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a90a-330a-7132-bdeb-2012149a0d8b"},"sequence":1552,"recorded_at":1780531400003099,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e30341db-b4fb-4a21-89c3-ee429bb9c3cc","payload_type":"run.terminal.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_terminal","command_id":"e30341db-b4fb-4a21-89c3-ee429bb9c3cc","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e30341db-b4fb-4a21-89c3-ee429bb9c3cc"},"terminal":"completed","text":"R6 work is handed off to review (board `to-review`, checklist 19/19).\n\nWhat this run did: completed the preserved R6 canonical-lease fix for R5-F1 by correcting two defects — `ensureNoAlias` treated system symlinks (`/var`, `/tmp`) as Finder aliases and refused every temp-root install, which also caused the validation hang (refused download → no install path → config never starts → the latch wait suspends forever); and the alias regression now uses a bounded 30s latch wait plus a pre-latch gate check, so a config that never starts becomes a reported failure with Flow diagnostics. The regression steps and both assertions are otherwise identical to the rev5 attack source.\n\nEvidence: full Core suite exit 0 with 157 passed, Scripts tests exit 0 (4 OK), xcodebuild BUILD SUCCEEDED, and narrowing mutant M30 (spelling-only lease key) killed by the alias test with the exact R5-F1 signature while the same-spelling control stayed green; the mutant was reverted with sha256 verified. Outcome artifact `TASK-260916-13diw4_outcome_v6.md` is attached. The candidate is left uncommitted in the story worktree with no producer commit, generator inputs untouched, and no production runner operations.","reason":null}}
